\title{Continuous Images Preserve Compactness}
\taxon{Theorem}
\import{macros}
\p{
    If #{X} is [compact](def-005R) and #{f:X\to Y} is continuous, then the
    image #{f(X)}, with its subspace topology, is compact.
}
\proof{
    Regard #{f} as a map #{\bar f:X\to f(X)}. It is continuous because the
    preimage of a subspace-open set #{f(X)\cap O} is #{f^{-1}(O)}, which is
    open in #{X}.

    Let #{\mathcal{U}} be an open cover of #{f(X)}. The collection
    ##{
        \{\bar f^{-1}(U)\mid U\in\mathcal{U}\}
    }
    is an open cover of #{X}. Choose a finite subcover
    #{\bar f^{-1}(U_1),\ldots,\bar f^{-1}(U_n)}. Surjectivity of #{\bar f}
    then implies that #{U_1,\ldots,U_n} covers #{f(X)}.
}
